---
import SiteLayout from '@/layouts/SiteLayout.astro'
import { withBase } from '@/data/register'
import { platoPortrait } from '@/data/dossier-art'
---

<SiteLayout current="methodology" title="How we choose one product" description="How Platonic Ideal researches a category, weighs evidence, and chooses one product—or explains why no product qualifies." breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', href: withBase() }, { name: 'Our approach' }]}>
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="editorial-hero">
      <div class="method-intro">
        <figure class="method-intro-art">
          <img src={withBase(platoPortrait.src)} width={platoPortrait.width} height={platoPortrait.height} alt={platoPortrait.alt} loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>Plato · {platoPortrait.holdingInstitution}<br /><a href={platoPortrait.sourceUrl} rel="external">Image source ↗</a></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="method-intro-copy">
          <p class="label">How we choose</p>
          <h1 class="editorial-title">One clear answer.</h1>
          <p class="editorial-dek">Plato began with a question that still matters here: before we compare many examples, can we say what the thing is for? Platonic Ideal brings that search for the ideal into everyday buying.</p>
          <p class="editorial-dek">Most product reviews give you a list and leave the decision to you. We define the category, test how an object is built and fails, and use one public standard to choose one product—or explain why no product qualifies.</p>
          <p class="methodology-authority"><a href="../constitution/">Read our full rules <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> · the standard behind every decision.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="method-grid">
      <nav class="method-nav" aria-label="Methodology sections">
        <a href="#rule">01 · The answer</a>
        <a href="#admission">02 · The questions</a>
        <a href="#evidence">03 · Evidence</a>
        <a href="#empty">04 · No pick</a>
        <a href="#revocation">05 · Updates</a>
      </nav>

      <article>
        <section class="method-section" id="rule">
          <span class="label">§ 01 · The answer</span>
          <h2>One clear answer—or no answer yet.</h2>
          <p>A category gets one named product when the evidence supports it. We do not hand you a “top five” and call that a decision. The point is to reduce the research burden while showing you exactly how we made the call.</p>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Our pick</strong><span>One specific product met the standard and is named here.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>No qualifying pick</strong><span>We looked seriously but did not find one product that earned the answer.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Still researching</strong><span>The evidence is not complete enough for a final recommendation.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>What we never do</strong><span>No “top five,” tie, preference matrix, or paid ranking.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="admission">
          <span class="label">§ 02 · The questions</span>
          <h2>Five questions every product must answer.</h2>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>01 · Is the category clear?</strong><span>It must represent one coherent job, not several incompatible ones.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>02 · Does the core hold up?</strong><span>The useful body must survive ordinary wear rather than invite early replacement.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>03 · What happens when it fails?</strong><span>Expected failures must be repairable, rebuildable, maintainable, or rationally renewable.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>04 · Will this model stay identifiable?</strong><span>The product needs a stable specification, supported lineage, or durable parts ecosystem.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>05 · Does it remove the need to keep comparing?</strong><span>It should reach the practical ceiling of the category without relying on luxury theater.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="evidence">
          <span class="label">§ 03 · Evidence</span>
          <h2>Show us how it lasts.</h2>
          <p>A product earns the pick through multiple independent signals: material and construction, field longevity, spare-parts access, published service paths, professional adoption, production continuity, or a warranty that genuinely restores use. “Premium,” “iconic,” and “built to last” are descriptions—not evidence.</p>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Useful evidence</strong><span>Replaceable parts, repair manuals, known failure mechanisms, production continuity, and real service paths.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Warning signs</strong><span>Sealed construction, proprietary lock-in, disposable coatings, and irreversible degradation.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="empty">
          <span class="label">§ 04 · No pick</span>
          <h2>We say no only after a serious rescue pass.</h2>
          <p>“No qualifying pick” does not mean we stopped looking. Before we leave a category empty, we clarify the job, separate incompatible uses, distinguish essential flaws from preferences, and test whether maintenance, repair, rebuilding, or rational replacement changes the answer. Then we explain what a future product would need to do.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="revocation">
          <span class="label">§ 05 · Updates</span>
          <h2>We change our mind in public.</h2>
          <p>A product leaves the catalog if its construction changes, parts disappear, serviceability collapses, evidence proves weaker than claimed, or another product better meets the same standard. We keep the history because changing a verdict is part of maintaining trust.</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
